# The Best Mac Note Taking Apps in 2026: 7 Compared, Free and Paid

By [Samarth Wagh](https://lynkk.ai/blog/authors/samarth-wagh) · 4 November 2026 · App prices checked against each vendor's own pricing page in November 2026

> **Quick answer:** For most people the best Mac note taking app is **Apple Notes**: free, already installed, and it now records and transcribes audio inside a note. **Bear** at **$2.99** a month is the nicest place to write, **Obsidian** is free and keeps plain files, and **Goodnotes Pro** at **$35.99** a year is the one for handwriting.

A Mac note taking app has to do three unglamorous things well: open fast, sync reliably, and let you find a note you wrote eight months ago. Most of the arguments online are about features that matter far less than those three.

> **Disclosure:** Lynkk makes a meeting recorder, listed last. It isn't a general note-taking app, and we say so plainly. Competitor prices come from each vendor's own pricing page, checked in November 2026.

## Our quick picks

| App | Best for | Price |
|---|---|---|
| Apple Notes | Most people, free and built in | Free with macOS |
| Bear | Writing in markdown | Free tier; Pro $2.99 a month or $29.99 a year |
| Obsidian | Plain files you own | Free for personal use; Sync $5 a month |
| Notion | Teams and databases | Free; Plus $10 a member a month |
| Craft | Documents that look good | Free; Plus $6.40 a month |
| Goodnotes | Handwriting and iPad | Free tier; Pro $35.99 a year |
| Lynkk | Meeting notes written for you | Free; Pro $15 a month |

## What is the best Mac note taking app overall?

The best notes app for Mac is Apple Notes for most people, Bear for writers, Obsidian for people who want plain files, and Goodnotes for handwriting. All 7 note taking apps for Mac below run natively on macOS. Here's what each one does well, and where it falls down.

### 1. Apple Notes: best for most people

Apple Notes is free, already on your Mac, and syncs to your iPhone with no setup. Apple's guide lists folders, tags, Smart Folders, document scanning, shared notes, links, and audio recording inside a note with live, searchable transcripts, [per Apple's Notes guide](https://support.apple.com/guide/notes/welcome/mac).

That transcription feature quietly makes it a stronger note taking app for meetings than it used to be. What it lacks is backlinks, plugins and markdown export.

- **Pick it if:** you want zero setup and everything on Apple devices.
- **Look elsewhere if:** you want markdown files or plugins.

### 2. Bear: best for writing in markdown

The Bear app is a markdown editor that looks good enough to make you want to write in it, with tags instead of folders. The free tier covers local notes, document scanning and 3 themes, while Pro adds iCloud sync and search inside PDFs and images for $2.99 a month or $29.99 a year after a 7-day trial, per [Bear's site](https://bear.app/). It runs on Mac, iPhone and iPad.

- **Pick it if:** you write a lot and live inside Apple's ecosystem.
- **Look elsewhere if:** you need Windows, Android or a web app.

### 3. Obsidian: best for owning your notes

Obsidian stores every note as a markdown file in a folder on your disk, so the notes survive the app. It's free for personal use with no limits, Sync is $5 a user a month, and commercial use asks for a $50 a year licence, per [Obsidian's pricing](https://obsidian.md/pricing).

- **Pick it if:** you want local files, backlinks and plugins.
- **Look elsewhere if:** you want something polished on day one.

### 4. Notion: best for teams and databases

Notion is a note taking app that became a workspace, which is either the appeal or the problem depending on what you want. Free covers personal use, Plus is $10 and Business $20 a member a month, with up to 20% off yearly, per [Notion's pricing](https://www.notion.com/pricing).

- **Pick it if:** your notes are shared or structured as databases.
- **Look elsewhere if:** you want fast local capture and offline reliability.

### 5. Craft: best for documents that look good

Craft sits between a notes app and a document editor, and it's the one to pick when notes get shared outside your team. Free covers 1,500 blocks, 1GB of storage and a single space, while Plus is $6.40 a month, per [Craft's pricing](https://craft.do/pricing). It syncs across iPhone, iPad, Mac and web.

- **Pick it if:** your notes become documents other people read.
- **Look elsewhere if:** 1,500 blocks won't hold your archive and you don't want to pay.

### 6. Goodnotes: best for handwriting

Goodnotes is the handwriting notes app for Mac and iPad, built around writing with an Apple Pencil rather than typing. The free plan is limited to 3 files and 5MB imports. Essential is $11.99 a year, Pro is $35.99 a year and Teams is $120 a seat a year, with an AI Pass add-on at $9.99 a month, per [Goodnotes' pricing](https://www.goodnotes.com/pricing).

- **Pick it if:** you take handwritten notes in lectures or meetings.
- **Look elsewhere if:** you type everything and never use a stylus.

### 7. Lynkk: best for notes you don't type

Every app above assumes you're typing. The notes you never get are the ones from meetings, where you're talking instead of writing.

Lynkk records Zoom, Google Meet and Teams calls, or a conversation in the room from the Mac app, then writes up [meeting notes](https://lynkk.ai/features/meeting-notes) with action items and decisions. On Pro, Ask Lynkk answers questions across every note with the minute behind each answer. Free includes unlimited recordings and the first 5 hours of AI notes, with Pro at $15 a month, per [Lynkk's pricing](https://lynkk.ai/pricing).

- **Pick it if:** most of what you'd want to remember gets said out loud.
- **Look elsewhere if:** you want one app for articles, ideas and to-dos. Lynkk isn't that, and it works alongside one rather than replacing it.

## What is the best free note taking app for Mac?

Apple Notes is the strongest free note taking app for Mac, because there's no tier to outgrow and it's already installed. Obsidian is free for personal use with no feature limits, and Bear, Craft and Goodnotes all have free tiers with caps.

Watch where each free tier stops: Craft limits you to 1,500 blocks, Goodnotes to 3 files, and Bear holds back iCloud sync. Apple Notes and Obsidian are the two with no meaningful ceiling.

## Which Mac note taking app is best for students?

For students, Goodnotes suits handwritten lecture notes on an iPad, while Apple Notes or Obsidian suit typed notes you'll search later. The deciding factor is usually whether you write by hand.

If you record lectures, check the rules at your institution first, then use something that transcribes. Apple Notes can record and transcribe inside a note, which covers most lecture cases without another app.

## Which app is best for meeting notes on a Mac?

For meeting notes, pick an app that captures what was said rather than one you type into while half-listening. Apple Notes with audio recording works for small meetings, and a dedicated recorder works better for calls with several people.

The test is simple: after a 45-minute call, do you have the decisions and who owns what? If your notes stop at minute 10 because the conversation got interesting, the app isn't the problem, the typing is.

## How do you choose a Mac note taking app?

Choose on capture speed, search and export, in that order. Everything else is preference, and preference is why these debates never end.

1. **Capture:** how fast from thought to saved note?
2. **Search:** does it find a phrase from last year?
3. **Export:** can you leave with your notes in a readable format?
4. **Sync:** does it work on your phone, and offline?
5. **Price:** what does it cost once sync is included?

If you want the system rather than the app, our guide to [personal knowledge management](https://lynkk.ai/blog/personal-knowledge-management) covers PARA and Zettelkasten, and the [best second brain apps](https://lynkk.ai/blog/best-second-brain-apps) comparison goes deeper on linked-note tools.

## FAQ

### Is Apple Notes good enough as a note taking app?

For most people, yes. Apple Notes is free, fast, syncs across Apple devices, and supports folders, tags, Smart Folders, scanning and shared notes. It can also record audio in a note with a searchable transcript. The main gaps are backlinks, plugins and markdown export, which matter to a minority of users.

### Which Mac notes app works offline?

Apple Notes, Bear, Obsidian and Craft all work offline, though sync resumes when you reconnect. Obsidian is the most independent, because notes are plain files already on your disk. Notion is the weakest offline, since it's built around a hosted workspace rather than local storage.

### What is the best note taking app for a MacBook Air?

The same apps that suit any Mac, because note taking isn't demanding on hardware. Apple Notes, Bear and Obsidian all run comfortably on a MacBook Air. If you want handwriting, you'll need an iPad with an Apple Pencil, since the Air has no touchscreen or stylus support.

### Can you handwrite notes on a Mac?

Not directly, because Macs have no touchscreen or stylus. Handwriting apps like Goodnotes run on Mac but are designed for iPad with an Apple Pencil, with the Mac version used mainly for reading and organising. Many people write on the iPad and review the same notes on their Mac.

### Which note taking app syncs between Mac and iPhone?

Apple Notes syncs through iCloud with no setup. Bear syncs with iCloud on Pro, Craft syncs across iPhone, iPad, Mac and web, Notion syncs through its own cloud, and Obsidian syncs with its paid service or a folder-sync tool of your choice. All of them cover the Mac-to-iPhone case.

### Do you need a paid note taking app?

Usually not. Apple Notes and Obsidian cover most needs for free, and the paid tiers mostly buy sync, collaboration or AI. Pay when you hit a real limit, such as Craft's 1,500-block cap or Bear's iCloud sync, rather than because a feature list looks impressive.

## Conclusion

Apple Notes for most people, Bear for writers, Obsidian for plain files, Craft for shared documents, Goodnotes for handwriting. Then decide how meetings get captured, because that's the half of your notes no app above will type for you.

**Try Lynkk.** It writes the meeting notes you were too busy talking to take. [Start for free](https://lynkk.ai/).

